WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Freedom Force, a frighteningly real tale of international terror.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:27.680
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.640
I'm Mike. I'm Sam.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.440
I'm Sean.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:37.380
And I'm Joe. Guys, today we are talking about Freedom Force, apparently based on a true story.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.820
You didn't hear about that?

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.380
Yeah, I guess I didn't hear about that, but I don't know, maybe it was.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.440
It absolutely was not.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.980
I think that's a little weird for the back of the box, but that's the risk that we take,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.240
reading the backs of boxes.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.560
We never know what we're going to get, and that's what we got this time.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.980
But before we start talking about Freedom Force as a game and everything like that, I think

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.700
that we should cover something that has been on everyone's mind.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.260
And that is the NES Zapper in general.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:12.400
Where has it been? We haven't had a game in a while, and when we do have a game, we had the gotcha game.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:13.760
Oh, the sport.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.320
Yeah, gotcha the sport.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.460
Yeah, you remember. They're usually reduced to these short, gimmicky, minigame-style stuff.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.340
What's our feelings on the Zapper overall?

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.100
I think it's run its course.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:30.900
Um, I think that by this time in the NES's life cycle, it should have been laid to rest.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.420
Um, but you know, here we are.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.560
I think that there was more potential that they didn't tap into.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.640
I mean, as far as I know, they never really get too big with the Zapper again.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.280
What are you looking for next in a Zapper game?

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.160
Well, I know that the one suggestion that I think I've said before is having like one

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.520
player controlling a character while the other player covers for them.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.480
I think that's cool. Or just other ways to incorporate it.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.440
I mean, I don't have a lot off the top of my head, but I do think that it's just so accurate.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.420
I mean, there are so many options of using a gun.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.600
Like, don't take that out of context.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.880
It's just a gun that you aim at your TV.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.500
There are so many possibilities you can do with it.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.920
And I feel like they are just for some reason not.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.580
Yeah, and also like for the Zapper game that you're describing with like a duck and cover

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.920
system, I would have liked like an NES pedal accessory that you could put your foot down

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.940
on to cover and then take off of it.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.400
Okay, now we're getting into murkier waters.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.660
So what else could the pedal be used for?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.660
Well, the pedal could be used for racing games.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.700
Pedal racing games with a Zapper.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.660
So it's like GTA where you have the Zapper in the car, first person, and you're trying

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.480
to shoot people in other cars as they come next to you.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.280
I just wanted to remind the driving Zapper game.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.420
That sounds pretty cool. Yeah.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.340
So far, most of the games that we have are like...

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.520
You shoot to the left to turn left.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.540
Yeah. Well, almost all the games that we have other than Gumshoe are from like the first person

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.120
perspective of like you either being like frozen in time, like you're definitely not allowed

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.800
to even look around, or you're not in control of when you can look.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:08.260
True. And Freedom Force is made by Sunsoft, who is a company that we haven't heard much from.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.440
They made Spy Hunter.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.640
Mm-hmm. Sean was pretty hip on that.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.600
I like Spy Hunter.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.140
Sam? You're shrugging your shoulders over there?

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.240
But it's positive shrug.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.160
Okay, that's good. I think that's one I did not play.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.280
Okay. Yeah, I don't think I was not.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.020
Interesting. You never even saw it in like the arcade or as like a HTML5 game?

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.080
Like, it's definitely like an interesting web game.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.760
If you haven't played Spy Hunter, you might remember, or maybe not, this might have just

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.960
been a me and Sean episode, Sky Kid.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.360
Was Sky Kid just me and you?

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.660
I don't remember, but Sky Kid was that game where like you immediately take off and crash.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.980
Oh, yeah. It was a shmup that went from right to left, like a manga.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.940
You're right. And Sky Kid was, it's weird.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.340
I said Sunsoft, but that's really more like a Namco game.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:59.660
Yeah. However, Sky Kid apparently like in the NES version credits Sunsoft.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.260
So maybe they like either published it or helped with like converting the Famicom cart

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.000
to an NES cart, but that doesn't seem like a hard thing to do.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.220
So I don't know how they get credit for this, even though it's totally a Namco property.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.920
They will also go on to, if you remember our mascot discussion in a previous episode

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.740
that I can't remember.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.740
Oh, what's it called? They will go on to develop Arrow the Acrobat for the Super NES.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.940
Oh, okay. Yeah. The Acrobrat or Acrobat?

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.680
Acrobat. Okay. He's a bat.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.140
He's a literal. Acrobrat would have been cool.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.000
No, he's a literal bat.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.700
Oh, he's a literal bat. That makes more sense.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.640
Yeah. That's the pun. Okay.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:37.200
That's the pun. And then in the manual for Freedom Force, just because we're on the Sunsoft topic, they put

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.960
out like, you know, don't miss these Sunsoft games.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.240
And it's like Sky Kid and Freedom Force.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.580
And then it's like Platoon and Xenophone.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.700
And I'm like, what are those games?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.060
I looked them up.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.000
We won't get those until December of 1988.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.460
Well, I mean, keep an eye out.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.240
That's not that bad. Yeah, keep an eye out, I guess.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.120
But they're making them seem like they're available for purchase.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.580
So I guess they just like printed these.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.860
I guess also you'd think people weren't all buying this day one.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.480
Maybe like six months later.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.600
Now it's a lot closer to what that's going on.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.880
They should have just put their entire catalog for the next 20 years in the manual.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.960
They should have put Duke Nukem Forever in there.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.520
Right. Here's our 20-year plan for video games.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.180
And just invest now and get in early on Sunsoft.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.760
The crown for. I don't know what happened to Sunsoft.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:21.980
I don't know if they're still around. They're still around.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.340
It doesn't sound familiar to me.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.300
What do you think?

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:30.380
I mean, I don't know what they've developed, but the Wikipedia page uses the present tense.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.080
There you go. That's all I'm sold.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:33.800
Yeah. They don't have to make anything.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.540
Exactly. If they're not dead on Wikipedia, they're truly not dead.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.920
Okay. Let's get into Freedom Force here.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.600
Can someone give me an overview of the game in like a sentence?

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.320
A sentence is challenging.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.860
There might be a couple extra sentences.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.640
Okay. What do you got? You play as these two action heroes.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.940
An airport is being hijacked.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.180
And you basically scroll through similar to paintball?

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.840
Or what was it called? It's called Gotcha the Sport.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.840
Gotcha the Sport. And you shoot at the enemies as they come out while trying to avoid the innocent people.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.860
I approve of that message.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.520
It was four, according to Sam, four sentences.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.440
But I approve of it. There were some interjections, but I'll allow it.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.180
And I think the only thing I would add is that you are anti-terrorist gunmen.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.960
So you're like trying to stop a terrorist attack in an airport.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.720
It's important to note that you are anti-terrorist.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:30.640
Well, the first couple times I played it, I was only shooting at the innocent people because

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.460
I didn't realize I was anti-terrorist.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:39.880
Before we go any further, I would just like to say that this has the most bitchin' intro video.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.640
Thank you. Of NES games so far.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.440
Of many games.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.560
Yeah. I mean, it's got cinematography on point.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:54.180
It's got some really good sound for what it was working with.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.000
Yeah. No, absolutely. Pan up that building.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.640
Which I feel like everyone always talks about one of the Mega Man.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:06:59.560
Was it Mega Man 2?

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.080
I mean, you were like, the camera pans up.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.100
This one did it first.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:08.340
Yeah. And I think I was reading about this game a lot before I actually decided to play it.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.960
So I got an overview of it.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.520
And to be honest, it kind of sounded like Hogan's Alley to me as I was reading about it.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.000
And then I booted up the game and I was just not expecting this.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:22.900
The premise does not lend itself to this idea of like, the rest of the game really doesn't

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.380
lend itself to the idea that it's going to open like this.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:28.860
I'm actually still a little not sure on what's happening in the cutscene.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.560
There's like a helicopter gets shot down.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:32.120
Yeah. Who is that? Is that you?

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.580
No, it doesn't matter.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.780
Helicopter gets shot down and like a guy stands over it.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.920
Because then your guy, yeah, your guy stands over it.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.380
What's his name? Rex?

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.960
Rad Rex. And Manic Jackson is the other guy.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.160
There's a lot of 80s tropes going on here.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.540
You got Rad Rex and Manic Jackson are like player one and player two.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.520
They're part of the Gorilla Terminator squad.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.220
Which definitely just sounds like Terminator, you know, the James Cameron film.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.940
It's like, oh, that's doing well. So let's put that in our thing.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:09.860
And then, you know, that intro and the sound in general just kind of like, it just all feels

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.900
like as many 80s tropes as they can squeeze into this.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:16.740
I'm surprised that there's no Rambo like references going on.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.440
But that game is coming out in like three episodes.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:20.580
So we have that.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:28.100
Basically, even in between levels, you'll get this very, it's kind of redundant because

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.700
it keeps showing it to you, but it's your profile, I guess, your Terminator profile.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:37.100
But yeah, it lists your profession as a literal Terminator.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:38.880
Slash something else.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.980
Yeah, it goes by pretty fast.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.440
I'm never really able to like get all that information.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.300
It's a very fast paced game in general, I feel.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.880
But I did want to say that before we move away from this whole like awesome opening, that

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:56.360
even the splash screen title with the way Freedom Force looks and everything, like it's all

00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:02.840
pretty hype. And I'm not disappointed when I finally like shoot the zapper to continue on to actually

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.120
start the gameplay because the graphics inside the game are pretty damn good too.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:13.520
True. Right? I mean, you start off, you're on the runway and there's the airplane itself, right?

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.880
And then you see people coming out of like the airplane.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:17.660
The longest airplane that there's ever been.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.760
With a lot of doors. A lot of airplane doors.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:21.320
It's an emergency plane.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:26.860
Yeah, I guess. And I just thought like that looked really nice compared to what I was expecting.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:31.900
Not as nice as the opening intro video, but I think there's something to be said about

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:37.760
being able to clearly recognize like, oh, that person is an enemy and that person is someone

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:38.940
who's held against their will.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.060
They use color pretty well in that regard.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.020
And that person is just someone in a bikini who's just hanging out here.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.940
That's later, but they can't be shot.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.340
So like they're just literally just in the game, not even as like an obstacle you're supposed

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.580
to be careful with their shoes, just in the game for like, I don't know.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:55.060
I think that they're just a strike.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.420
That's why I play NES games.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.020
But going off that, and I don't want to get us too off track, but I think it still counts

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:08.160
as like an introductory thing, but this game is way more mature than I expected out of

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.580
a Nintendo game at this point where there is blood.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.000
Do we see blood before?

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.580
We have not seen blood before.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:19.000
It explodes out of them and then they fall off the plane.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:24.100
Yeah, and I think I had something about this and I can't find it in my notes now, but there's

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:29.480
something about the way that the blood was done that they like got away with it because

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.940
it wasn't like a splatter effect.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:32.980
It kind of was a splatter.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.840
I think it looks more like a red dot to me.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:40.720
What? It's got like the splat thing.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.280
I'm looking at just a video right now.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.220
It's like a, it's definitely got a lot of tendrils going around.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.560
I guess I don't really remember it, so it couldn't have been that bad.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.640
If Joe's like frame freezing it, then that would probably look worse.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:59.180
Well, yeah, it's very tame compared to what we have now, but it's something that I didn't

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.740
see before in these games.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.140
I actually didn't even register that.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.320
I was just like, oh yeah, it just looked normal, but I forgot that we've not really seen that

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.500
yet. Have we seen that at all?

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:08.900
Blood? Yeah.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.720
I think we might have seen it in one other game.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:11.640
I honestly can't remember.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:12.980
I mean, it wasn't even in Castlevania.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.160
Yeah, exactly. You know, so that's good.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.420
They had red hearts that you collect.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.500
And also, to talk about the violent nature of the game, is that sometimes when you shoot

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.480
enemies, the way that they die is different.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:30.660
So if you register a hit on an enemy, they might fall back or just kind of vanish.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:33.420
And then there's others that will fall forward on the airplane.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.840
Out of a window, yeah. And for a second, my mind thought, oh, that's a headshot.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.680
But then I was like, wait, the NES Zapper would not be able to detect.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:40.700
It's just one box.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:41.720
Yeah, it's just boxes.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:46.060
But I think that's pretty cool that they added a couple different variety of how enemies

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.760
will fall over. I think it depends on where they're coming out from.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.540
Got it. Well, that makes more sense, too.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.860
But I think it just helps out a lot more than in Duck Hunt, when you're shooting a duck,

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:59.800
it always is the same animation of the duck just suspended in a minute with a gotcha moment

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:01.340
and then falls down.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:02.360
A very Wile E. Coyote.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.840
Yeah, yeah. Which, I mean, lends itself to Duck Hunt.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:05.980
It's a very cartoony game.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.260
One thing that we haven't talked about yet is that you don't control where you move

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:17.340
in this game. It goes from left to right on its own, and you're kind of on guard knowing all the possibilities

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:22.080
of where enemies will come from, so you're going to have to kind of stalk out the position.

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:28.540
You're right. I misspoke now that I think of it when I talked about gotcha the sport earlier, because that

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:29.880
you control. You control that.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.780
You just sort of saunter at a leisurely pace automatically in this game.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.520
To be honest, I prefer this over gotcha.

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:41.580
Me too. I thought I wouldn't have, but I think I remember in the gotcha episode, I recall that I didn't

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:44.560
like the speed of movement, because it felt like...

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:45.620
And it was kind of arbitrary.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:50.120
Yeah, it just felt like a chore that you have to, with one hand, hold down your direction

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.500
that you want to go in, all to just go in one direction.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.680
There's no real prize for going backwards.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:59.260
If it had a reason for you to stop, or for you to go back, or wait, or something.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.040
Or get undercover, or something like that.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:07.520
Yeah, whereas this one, I thought sometimes the pacing of how it moves helps, because there's

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:12.140
times where five doors might be on the screen at one particular point, and you don't know

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.420
who's going to come out of which door.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.960
You have to be ready. And you have to be ready, because these guys shoot very fast.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:20.600
Am I wrong? Yeah. They're difficult.

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.360
You have to as much. It feels like you have to manage it a lot, but I thought it was just really interesting

